--- a/doc/Tcl.n
+++ b/doc/Tcl.n
@@ -94,14 +94,15 @@ substituted into the word in place of the brackets and all of the
 characters between them.
 There may be any number of command substitutions in a single word.
 Command substitution is not performed on words enclosed in braces.
-.IP "[8] \fBVariable substitution.\fR"
+.IP "[8] \fBVariable and expression substitution.\fR"
 If a word contains a dollar-sign
 .PQ $
 followed by one of the forms
 described below, then Tcl performs \fIvariable
-substitution\fR:  the dollar-sign and the following characters are
-replaced in the word by the value of a variable.
-Variable substitution may take any of the following forms:
+substitution\fR or \fIexpression substitution\fR:  the dollar-sign and the following characters are
+replaced in the word by the value of a variable or of an expression.
+Variable substitution may take any of the following three forms;
+the fourth form is expression substitution:
 .RS
 .TP 15
 \fB$\fIname\fR
@@ -136,6 +137,36 @@ or close brace characters,
 and \fIindex\fR can be any sequence of characters except for close brace
 characters.  No further
 substitutions are performed during the parsing of \fIname\fR.
+.TP 15
+\fB$((\fIexpression\fB))\fR
+.
+A dollar-sign followed by two open parentheses begins an
+\fIexpression substitution\fR, a shortcut for
+.QW "\fB[expr {\fIexpression\fB}]\fR" .
+The expression is the text between the two open parentheses and the first
+close parenthesis that balances the parentheses inside the text, and that
+close parenthesis must be followed immediately by a second one.
+Parentheses inside braces, double quotes or bracketed commands are not counted.
+.sp
+A \fB$((\fR that is not closed by a matching \fB))\fR is an error:
+\fB$((1+2)*3)\fR is rejected, and should be written \fB$(((1+2)*3))\fR
+or, with spaces, \fB$(( (1+2)*3 ))\fR.
+Any other form that begins with \fB$(\fR, such as \fB$( (1+2))\fR, is not an
+expression substitution; as before it is an array reference with an empty name.
+.sp
+The text is passed to \fBexpr\fR as if it were enclosed in braces:
+the Tcl parser performs no substitution on it, so that variable, command and
+backslash substitutions in it are performed once, by \fBexpr\fR, and the
+result of the expression is never substituted a second time.
+.sp
+For example,
+.QW "\fBset area $(( $width * $height ))\fR"
+sets \fBarea\fR to the product of the two variables, and
+.QW "\fBputs \N'34'n=$(( 2**10 ))!\N'34'\fR"
+prints
+.QW "\fBn=1024!\fR" .
+Extra parentheses around the expression make no difference:
+\fB$(( $a+$b ))\fR and \fB$(( ($a+$b) ))\fR have the same value.
 .PP
 There may be any number of variable substitutions in a single word.
 Variable substitution is not performed on words enclosed in braces.
